---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { P } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers();
const { m, pc, PAY, PEN } = h;
const E = P.afc.england;
const ex = h.nhsNet('england', PAY.afcPay('england', '5', 0));
const hc = PAY.hcas(PAY.afcPay('england', '5', 0), 'inner');
const T = PEN.tierTable('nhsEW');
const sources = Object.entries(P.sources) as Array<[string, { url: string; label: { en: string } }]>;
---
<PageLayout title="Pay Band Calc Method: Scales, Tax, Pension Tiers and Tests" description="Pay Band Calc method: how NHS and teacher take-home pay and pensions for 2026/27 are worked out, the order of deductions, pension rules, tests and gaps." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the pay, take-home and pension figures are worked out', description: 'Method of Pay Band Calc', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the figures are worked out</h1><p class="mt-3 text-lg text-navy-700">Four tested engines (pay, tax, pension and take-home) read one parameter file. This page sets out what each one does, the checks it passes and what it leaves out. Sources read on <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}</time>.</p></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2>1. Pay scales</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Agenda for Change.</strong> Every band and point of the {E.year} scales for England (NHS Employers, {displayDate(E.published, 'en-GB')}), Wales (pay letter AfC(W) 02/2026) and Scotland (gov.scot, updated {displayDate(P.afc.scotland.updated, 'en-GB')}), and the {P.afc.ni.year} scale for Northern Ireland, the latest published. Each point carries the years before the next one where the scale gives them.</li>
      <li><strong>High Cost Area Supplement.</strong> A percentage of basic pay, rounded up to the pound, then held between the minimum and maximum of the zone. With that rounding, band 5 entry in inner London gives {m(PAY.afcPay('england', '5', 0) + hc)}, the figure NHS Employers prints.</li>
      <li><strong>Hourly, unsocial hours and overtime.</strong> England hourly rates as published, enhancements by band from section 2 of the NHS terms and conditions handbook, overtime at time and a half and double time on public holidays for bands 1 to 7, plain time for part-time staff up to 37.5 hours.</li>
      <li><strong>Teachers.</strong> England: the advisory points of the School Teachers’ Pay and Conditions Document 2026 for September 2026 and September 2027, in all four pay areas, with the leadership, head teacher group, leading practitioner and unqualified ranges and the allowances. Scotland: the SNCT salary tables. Wales: the 2025 Welsh pay document. Northern Ireland: the determination of September 2025. An award announced but not printed in a scale is shown as still to come, never estimated.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>2. Take-home pay</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pension contribution.</strong> The tier is found from annual pensionable pay, and its rate is applied to all of it. Pensionable pay includes the London supplement and unsocial hours payments for NHS staff. For part-time staff and teachers the tier uses the pay actually earned.</li>
      <li><strong>Income tax.</strong> On pay after the contribution (net pay arrangement). Personal Allowance of {m(P.tax.personalAllowance)}, withdrawn by £1 for each £2 above {m(P.tax.taperThreshold)}. Rest of the UK: 20, 40 and 45 %. Scotland: six rates from 19 to 48 %, the bands measured on taxable income above the allowance.</li>
      <li><strong>National Insurance.</strong> 8 % between {m(P.tax.ni.primaryThreshold)} and {m(P.tax.ni.upperEarningsLimit)}, 2 % above, on pay before the pension, worked on an annual basis.</li>
      <li><strong>Student loans.</strong> Plans 1, 2, 4 and 5 at 9 % and the postgraduate loan at 6 % above HMRC’s 2026/27 thresholds, on pay before the pension.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Example: a band 5 at entry in England, {m(PAY.afcPay('england', '5', 0))}, pays {m(ex.pension)} in pension ({pc(ex.pensionRate)}), {m(ex.tax)} in income tax and {m(ex.ni)} in NI, and takes home {m(ex.net)} a year. The figures are annual and divided by twelve; a real payslip works month by month, which can shift a pound or two.</p>
    <h2>3. Pension estimates</h2>
    <p>The NHS Pension Scheme 2015 adds 1/{h.num(P.pension.nhsEW.accrual)} of each year’s pensionable pay and revalues the total by CPI plus {pc(P.pension.nhsEW.realRevaluation)} while you are active (NHSBSA). The Teachers’ Pension Scheme adds 1/{h.num(P.pension.tps.accrual)} (regulation 53 of the 2014 regulations) and revalues by CPI plus {pc(P.pension.tps.realRevaluation)}. The projection works in today’s money: each year’s slice grows by the real revaluation only. Normal pension age is the State Pension age with a floor of {h.num(P.pension.npaMin)} (Public Service Pensions Act 2013, section 10). A lump sum is £{h.num(P.pension.tps.commutation)} for each £1 a year of pension given up, capped for teachers at 30/7 of the pension (Teachers’ Pensions factsheet).</p>
    <p>The contribution tables cover six schemes from April 2026; in England and Wales the NHS table has {h.num(T.length)} tiers, from {pc(T[0].rate)} to {pc(T.at(-1)!.rate)}.</p>
    <h2>4. Tests</h2>
    <p>Every build runs the engine tests and stops if one fails. They check, among others: band 5 entry in inner London at the NHS Employers figure; HCAS minimum and maximum; M1 and inner London U3 against the pay document; income tax at {m(30000)} and {m(60000)} in the rest of the UK and in Scotland; the Personal Allowance reaching nothing at {m(P.tax.personalAllowance * 2 + P.tax.taperThreshold)}; NHSBSA and Teachers’ Pensions tier boundaries to the penny; one and two years of career average accrual; the lump sum cap; and that the pension comes off before tax but not before NI or a student loan. Each content page is also built in strict mode: a link to a page that does not exist stops the build.</p>
    <h2>5. What is left out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your tax code, if it is not the standard one; salary sacrifice schemes; on-call payments and bank shifts.</li>
      <li>Pension estimates for the Scottish and Northern Ireland schemes, the NHS maximum lump sum, early retirement reductions and the annual allowance: no published figure was found that this site could cite.</li>
      <li>The 2026/27 Agenda for Change scale for Northern Ireland and the September 2026 teacher scales of Wales and Northern Ireland, not published on {displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}.</li>
      <li>Hourly rates for Scotland and Wales, which their pay pages do not publish.</li>
      <li>No figure is advice, and the site does not know your pension record.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>6. Sources</h2>
    <p>The parameter file cites {sources.length} official pages and documents, read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}:</p>
    <ul class="columns-1 text-sm md:columns-2">{sources.map(([, s]) => <li><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">{s.label.en}</a></li>)}</ul>
    <p>Errors: see the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> and the <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
